0
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,960
MCP deleted its own handshake. The July 2002 6 spec removes the handshake. It also removes

1
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:07,200
the session ID.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,360
So, how does the server know who's asking?

3
00:00:09,983 --> 00:00:13,503
A station works like a wooden key card, good for one room.

4
00:00:13,503 --> 00:00:15,983
Now every request shows a passport.

5
00:00:15,983 --> 00:00:19,183
Inside are its protocol version and client capabilities.

6
00:00:19,183 --> 00:00:19,983
Around the world.

7
00:00:19,967 --> 00:00:22,567
gets an unsupported version error.

8
00:00:22,567 --> 00:00:28,087
Need state across calls, the server creates a handle, the client passes it as a normal tool

9
00:00:28,087 --> 00:00:29,807
argument, illustrative.

10
00:00:29,951 --> 00:00:34,111
a tool needs the user to choose. The server returns an input required result.

11
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:37,631
Then the client sends the same request again with the answers.

12
00:00:38,191 --> 00:00:41,231
Roots, sampling and learning.

13
00:00:39,935 --> 00:00:42,855
blogging or deprecated, but still work.

14
00:00:42,855 --> 00:00:45,575
So each request explains itself.

15
00:00:45,575 --> 00:00:47,775
State becomes explicit handles.

16
00:00:47,775 --> 00:00:49,015
Questions become a retry loop.

17
00:00:49,986 --> 00:00:51,666
miss the handshake, tell me below.

